[
  {
    "question": "Two design documents disagree about who checks a task's text (empty, more than one line, longer than 200 characters). The API design says the service checks it and answers with one of three coded reasons, which the screen uses to show each message under the field. The architecture design says the request-validation layer rejects it first, which answers with a generic error list that has no code (as the guestbook does). Which one holds? (Decides findings COH-design-1 and COH-design-2.)",
    "allow_free_text": true,
    "blocks": ["COH-design-1", "COH-design-2"],
    "blocking": false,
    "options": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "label": "The service checks it, with a coded reason",
        "recommended": true,
        "consequence": "The architecture design and one planned unit test are corrected to match; the API contract, the screen, the other tests and the decision record already assume this, and the three messages show under the field as designed."
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "label": "The validation layer rejects it, as the guestbook does",
        "recommended": false,
        "consequence": "The API design, the contract, the screen specification, the tests and one decision record are rewritten, and the screen loses the codes for these three messages and shows the generic list instead."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "The test plan puts the checks for the to-do list's own address and the main address in a new test file, frontend/src/router.test.tsx, but the list of files this change may touch leaves that file out, so the implementation step would be refused when it writes it. Add it to the list? (Decides finding COH-design-4.)",
    "allow_free_text": true,
    "blocks": ["COH-design-4"],
    "blocking": false,
    "options": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "label": "Add the file to the change's list",
        "recommended": true,
        "consequence": "The two address rules (R-5, parts 1 and 3) get their automated proof as planned."
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "label": "Leave it out",
        "recommended": false,
        "consequence": "Those two address rules ship with no automated proof until someone adds one."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "The to-do list's area description must list its black-box test scenario file, but that file is only written during implementation, and no implementation step may edit the area description. Whichever step lists it, one automated check fails for a while. When should the file be listed? (Decides finding COH-design-6.)",
    "allow_free_text": true,
    "blocks": ["COH-design-6"],
    "blocking": false,
    "options": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "label": "List it now, at the end of design",
        "recommended": true,
        "consequence": "The 'every listed file exists' check fails from now until the first implementation wave writes the file; it is declared in advance as an expected failure, the same kind of failure design and the work plan already carry (issue #314)."
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "label": "List it after implementation",
        "recommended": false,
        "consequence": "The 'every scenario file belongs to an area' check fails through the whole implementation stage, until the specification is reconciled at the end."
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "label": "Change the template first",
        "recommended": false,
        "consequence": "Let an implementation step list the file: that needs the template's maintainers (filed upstream), and this change waits for them or takes A or B meanwhile."
      }
    ]
  }
]

